# Cultural Landscape Report, Treatment & Management Plan: Little Round Top (National Park Service; Einhorn Yaffee Prescott, Rhodeside & Harwell, Hunter Research; 2 March 2012)
# Second reading of this report: chapter 4 (PDF pp. 115-128, "descriptions of the landscape by battle participants", each quoted by the report from a named account)
# and the chapter 2 and 3 passages added after the first reading (72-clr-lrt.xdef). Filed after 72-clr-lrt.xdef so that no earlier extract id moves.
# How to read these records: the quote is the report's text. A participant account is as good as its source; the claim says whose account it is and the state date carries the year
# of the account. Where the accounts disagree (the time at which the stone breastworks were built, and by whom) every reading is its own record with source certainty 'conflict'.
# The text layer lost the ti and tt glyphs (Ge ysburg, Na onal, ba le): every quote is typed with the groups ti tt tf ft tl ff ignored on both sides (text_defect ligature).
@ NPS-CLR-LRT

%basis Amos M. Judson, History of the Eighty-Third Regiment Pennsylvania Volunteers (Erie, 1865), p. 66 (note 1 of the report page)
115 | woodlots | Big Round Top wooded; the vale between the Round Tops treed but open and underbrushed | - | 1863-07-02 (account published 1865) | stated | The Eighty-Third Pennsylvania regimental history by Amos M. Judson (1865), quoted by the report, describes Big Round Top as a high rocky hill covered with woods and the vale between the two hills as about a hundred feet wide, covered with trees but rather open and underbrushed | =On the left was Big Round Top, a high, rocky hill, covered with woods and overlooking Little Round Top. Between the two hills there was a small vale (not a ravine as it is commonly called), about a hundred feet in width, and covered with trees, but rather open and underbrushed. | Amos M. Judson. >>> 1865. p.66.
115 | general | Little Round Top a rocky rise of loose boulders, low marshy ground in front and rear | - | 1863-07-02 (account published 1865) | stated | The same regimental history (Judson, 1865) describes the position as an irregular rocky rise of ground sloping down on two sides, front and rear, to low marshy ground, a huge solid rock covered with a thousand other loose boulders of every size and shape | =It was an irregular rocky rise of ground, sloping down on two sides (the front and rear) to low marshy ground, and might have been a hundred feet above its level. It consisted of a huge, solid rock, covered over with a thousand other loose boulders of every size and shape | Amos M. Judson. >>> 1865. p.66.

%basis Anonymous, Meehan's Monthly for March, quoted in the Gettysburg Compiler, 15 March 1892 (note 6 of the report page)
119 | woodlots | Large butternut tree on the summit said to have been General Warren's observation point | - | 1863-07-02 (account of 1892) | hedged | An anonymous post-war article, by what the report calls a purported witness, says General Warren's chief position was on the top of a large butternut tree which overlooked the other (the sentence is cut by a figure caption in the saved text and goes on to say the other trees on the top of the hill) | =The writer of this had the sad privilege [sic] of knowing that General Warren’s chief position was on the top of a large butternut tree, which overlooked the other | =An anonymous post-war article by a purported witness to the observation post of General G.K. Warren on Little Round Top remarked on the park’s forest and viewshed management efforts.

%basis John B. Bachelder, Gettysburg: What to See and How to See It (Boston, 1889), p. 66 (note 7 of the report page)
119 | woodlots | West face of Little Round Top recently cut at the time of the battle | - | 1863-07-02 (account of 1889) | stated | John B. Bachelder (1889), quoted by the report, wrote that at the time of the battle the wood had been recently cut away on the western face, which gave the rough naked sides of the artillery crowned crest the appearance of an immense battlement and a supremely good view | =At the time of the battle the wood had been recently cut away, on its western face, which gave the rough naked sides of this artillery crowned crest the appearance of an immense battlement, while the view of the battle from this spot was supremely good. | John B. Bachelder. Gettysburg: What to See >>> 1889. p.66.

%basis Report of Col. Joshua L. Chamberlain, 20th Maine Infantry, Report No. 196, 6 July 1863 (note 12 of the report page)
120 | earthworks | Rude shelters of loose rocks thrown up by the 20th Maine on July 2 | E-13 | 1863-07-02 | stated | Col. Joshua L. Chamberlain's report of 6 July 1863, quoted by the report, says that between brief intervals in the fighting rude shelters were thrown up of the loose rocks that covered the ground | =[Between brief intervals in the fighting] rude shelters were thrown up of the loose rocks that covered the ground. | Report of Col. Joshua L. Chamberlain, 20th Maine Infantry. >>> 6 July, 1863.

%basis Ellis Spear, "Recollections", attachment to Abbot Spear to Thomas J. Harrison, 28 February 1978 (note 13 of the report page)
122 | woodlots | Base and slopes between the Round Tops in oak with a sprinkling of pines; south side of Big Round Top thickly wooded | - | 1863-07-02 (recollection written after the war) | stated | Ellis Spear's recollection, quoted by the report, says the base and the slope on both sides were covered with oak trees, chiefly oak of perhaps a foot in diameter, with here and there a sprinkling of pines, boulders everywhere, and the steep side of Big Round Top thickly wooded two or three hundred yards to the south | =This base, as well as the slope on both sides is covered with oak trees, or chiefly with oak of perhaps a foot in diameter, more or less, with here and there a sprinkling of pines. The boulders were everywhere, some large and some small...I could see through the trees to the south, the steep and thickly wooded side of Big Round Top, two or three hundred yards distant. | Ellis Spear. >>> 28 February, 1978.
122 | fences | Fenced field east of Little Round Top with a lane bordered by worm fences | - | 1863-07-02 (recollection written after the war) | stated | Ellis Spear's recollection, quoted by the report, says that towards the rear (east) there was the same open forest and beyond it a fenced field, not visible from the position, with a lane crossing it bordered by worm fences | =Towards the rear the east, there was the same open forest and beyond was a fenced field not visible from our position with a lane crossing it bordered by ‘worm’ fences. | Ellis Spear. >>> 28 February, 1978.

%basis Frederick T. Lock to Joshua L. Chamberlain, 5 July 1886 (note 14 of the report page)
122 | roads | Wood road passing up from the rear of Little Round Top | - | 1863-07-02 (account of 1886) | stated | Frederick T. Lock wrote to Joshua L. Chamberlain in 1886 that when he first looked at the hill as a place for artillery he did not know there was a wood road passing up from the rear of the hill | =I said to the Captain, ‘what a grand place for artillery, but we cannot get guns up there,’ not knowing at the time that there was a wood road passing up from the rear of the hill. | Frederick T. Lock to Joshua L. Chamberlain, 5 July 1886.

%basis General S. W. Crawford, testimony before the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War (Washington, 1865), pp. 469-70 (note 15 of the report page)
122 | woodlots | Field of scrub oak near Little Round Top where the Pennsylvania Reserves division was massed | - | 1863-07-02 (testimony published 1865) | stated | General S. W. Crawford, testifying before Congress about the approach of his division to the base of Little Round Top, said he was directed to mass his command in a field of scrub oak, to the right | =He directed me to mass my command in a field of scrub oak, to the right, and hold them in readiness for any movement. | =Testifying before Congress, General S.W. Crawford described the approach of his division from Baltimore Pike to the scene of the fighting at the base of Little Round Top.

%basis Oliver Norton to Frank Huntington, 28 September 1888 (note 16 of the report page)
122 | woodlots | North slope of Little Round Top almost bare of vegetation | - | 1863-07-02 (account of 1888) | stated | Oliver Norton wrote in 1888 that on the northern slope of Little Round Top there was scarcely any vegetation and the ascent was not practicable for a man on horseback | =On the northern slope there was scarcely any vegetation, and the ascent was not practicable for a man on horseback. | Oliver Norton to Frank Huntington, 28 September, 1888.

%basis General G. K. Warren as quoted in Henry J. Hunt, "The Second Day at Gettysburg", Battles and Leaders of the Civil War, vol. 3 (New York, 1888), p. 307 (note 18 of the report page 123)
123 | woodlots | Long line of woods west of the Emmitsburg Road, a place for the enemy to form out of sight | - | 1863-07-02 (account of 1888) | stated | General Warren's recollection, quoted by the report, says the troops in the woods in front of Little Round Top could not see the ground before them and that the long line of woods on the west side of the Emmitsburg Road, which road was along the ridge, furnished an excellent place for the enemy to form out of sight | =could not see the ground in front of them, so that the enemy would come upon them before they would be aware of it. The long line of woods on the west side of the Emmitsburg Road (which road was along the ridge) furnished an excellent place for the enemy to form out of sight | @122: =General Gouvernor K. Warren, assigned to General Meade’s staff as its topographical engineer, recalled his initial assessment of Little Round Top.

%basis U.S. War Department, The War of the Rebellion: Official Records, Washington, 1889, vol. 27, p. 358 (note 19 of the report page 123)
123 | fences | Stone fences about the base of Little Round Top | - | 1863-07-02 (account in the Official Records) | stated | General James Longstreet's account, quoted by the report, says the Federals were driven back upon a commanding hill, precipitous and rough, and that numerous stone fences about its base added greatly to its strength, the enemy taking shelter behind these and holding them one after another | =soon dislodged and driven back upon a commanding hill, which is so precipitous and rough as to render it difficult of ascent. Numerous stone fences about its base added greatly to its strength. The enemy, taking shelter behind these, held them, one after another with great tenacity. | =General James Longstreet described the advance of his Confederate troops against Little Round Top.

%basis G. W. Hosmer, "The Battle of Thursday", New York Herald, 4 July 1863 (note 21 of the report page 123; the note prints the year as 1843)
123 | woodlots | Crest of Little Round Top covered with a growth of scrub oaks; low wood below | - | 1863-07-03 (newspaper account written the day after the fighting) | stated | New York Herald correspondent G. W. Hosmer, quoted by the report, described Little Round Top as not particularly precipitous but very rough and rocky on the front, the crest covered with a growth of scrub oaks and the divisions coming over rocks and through the low wood | =[Little Round Top] was not particularly precipitous, but on the front it was very rough and rocky, and the crest was covered with a growth of scrub oaks...[Hood’s] and McLaw’s [divisions came] over rocks and through the low wood. | =The day after fighting at Gettysburg ceased, New York Herald correspondent G.W. Hosmer described the conditions on Little Round Top.

%basis Frank M. Stoke to J. M. Stoke, 26 October 1863 (note 22 of the report page 123)
123 | general | Open, muddy and rocky ground between Rose Woods and Stony Ridge | - | 1863-10 (letter written after the battle about the ground crossed on July 2) | stated | A soldier of the First Pennsylvania Battalion, writing to his brother, described the ground between Rose Woods and Stony Ridge as an open field, blue swamp mud and very rocky | =Between [Rose] Woods and Stony Ridge is an open field, blue swamp mud and very rocky. This the Rebels had to cross in order to assault our works, which they did, in spite of all the grape and canister [sic] our men could pour into them. | =In a letter to his brother, a soldier in the 1st Pennsylvania Battalion described the swampy conditions at the base of Little Round Top.

%basis John P. Nicholson, ed., Pennsylvania at Gettysburg, vol. 1 (Harrisburg, 1893), pp. 520-21 (note 23 of the report page 124)
124 | general | Marsh and soft slough of Plum Run at the foot of Little Round Top | - | 1863-07-02 (account published 1893) | stated | A line officer of the 98th Pennsylvania, quoted by the report, wrote that the regiment charged through the marsh of Plum Run to the foot of the ridge on the west side of the swamp and that the difficulty of crossing the soft slough broke the line up and left men stuck in the mire | =We charged through the marsh of Plum Run, advancing to the foot of the ridge on the west side of the swamp; whatever there was in our immediate sight of the enemy, in our direct front, retreating before us with little firing; we however received a livelier fire from the left (Devil’s Den) while crossing the swamp, which, together with the difficulty of crossing through the soft slough, had the effect to break our line up somewhat, so that the halt at the foot of the ridge, though for a few moments only, allowed those who became delayed (stuck in the mire) in crossing, to catch up. | @123: =A line officer in the 98th Pennsylvania Infantry related the positions and actions of the unit during the closing hour of battle on July 2, 1863.

%basis Zack Landrum to his mother, 4 August 1863 (note 26 of the report page 124)
124 | fields | Wheat field about 500 yards wide crossed by the 4th Texas, then woods with a stone fence | - | 1863-07-02 (letter of August 1863) | stated | A soldier of the 4th Texas, in a later letter to his mother quoted by the report, says the regiment had to charge through a wheat field about 500 yards wide under the fire of about 100 guns, and after reaching the woods where there was a stone fence drove the Yankees from the fence and up the steep, rocky, heavy timbered side of the mountain | =[We] had to charge through a wheat field about 500 yards wide with the Yankee artillery about 100 guns firing on us. After reaching the woods where there was a stone fence, we run the Yankees from the fence and up the side of the mountain which was quite steep and covered all over with large rocks and heavy timber. | =In a later letter, the same soldier in the 4th Texas regiment of Robertson’s Brigade recounted his advance on Little Round Top.

%basis Samuel R. Hazen, "Fighting the Good Fight. The 140th N.Y. and Its Work on Little Round Top", The National Tribune, 13 September 1894 (note 28 of the report page 124)
124 | earthworks | Stone walls built in the dark by the 140th New York after burying their dead | E-14 | 1863-07-02 (night; account of 1894) | stated | A veteran quoted by the report from Samuel R. Hazen's article of 13 September 1894 on the 140th N.Y. and its work on Little Round Top says that after picking up and burying their dead they built stone walls in the dark to protect themselves from the sharpshooters the next day | =[We] picked up our dead and buried them; then we built stone walls in the dark to protect us from the sharpshooters the next day. | Samuel R. Hazen, “Fighting the Good Fight. >>> 13 September, 1894.

%basis Mary Genevie Green Brainard, Campaigns of the One Hundred and Forty-Sixth Regiment New York Volunteers (New York, 1915), pp. 112-13 (note 29 of the report page 125)
125 | earthworks | Rough stone wall ordered by Colonel Garrand, 146th New York | E-14 | 1863-07-02 (account published 1915) | stated | The regimental history of the One Hundred and Forty-Sixth New York (1915), quoted by the report, says the men went down the slope and stationed themselves behind the rocks that thickly strewed the hill and were instructed by Colonel Garrand to erect a rough stone wall for better protection | =As soon as the regiments [of Weed’s Brigade] took their positions, men from each went down the slope to the front and stationed themselves behind the rocks which thickly strewed the side of the hill. They were also instructed by Colonel Garrand to erect a rough stone wall to afford better protection for themselves. | Mary Genevie Green Brainard. >>> 1915. pp. 112-13

%basis Report of Col. Kenner Garrand, 146th Pennsylvania Infantry (so printed in the note), Report No. 217, 16 July 1863 (note 30 of the report page 125)
125 | earthworks | Stone wall built at night about half way down the slope, wherever the rocks gave no protection | E-14 | 1863-07-02 (night; report of 16 July 1863) | stated | Col. Kenner Garrand, in his report of 16 July 1863 (146th) quoted by the report, says that at night the naturally strong ridge was strengthened by building a stone wall about half way down the slope wherever the rocks offered no protection to the men | =At night this ridge, naturally strong, was strengthened by building a stone wall about half way down the slope, wherever the rocks offered no protection to the men. | Report of Col. Kenner Garrand, 146th Pennsylvania Infantry. >>> 16 July, 1863.

%basis Calvin Hamilton to A. M. Apsted, 10 October 1892, from the "Resolutions of Col. Hill" file of the park archives (note 31 of the report page 125)
125 | earthworks | Breastworks in front of the 16th Michigan said to have been built on the night of July 3 by Crawford's division, not there at the time of the battle | E-14 | 1863-07-03 (night; account of 1892) | conflict | Calvin Hamilton wrote on 10 October 1892, in a letter quoted by the report, that the stone breastworks in front of the 16th Michigan line were thrown up on the night of 3 July 1863 principally by Crawford's Division, that they were not there at the time of the battle on July 2 at that point, and that survivors of Crawford's division living at Gettysburg helped to throw them up; this differs from the accounts that put the building of stone works on the night of July 2 or the afternoon of July 2 | =[The stone breastworks in front of the line of the 16th Michigan Infantry] were thrown up on the night of the 3rd of July, 1863 principally by Crawford’s Division of the Fifth Corps. That they were not there at the time of the battle [on July 2] at that point is not disputed. We have survivors of Crawford’s division living here [in Gettysburg] who helped to throw up those breastworks on the night of the 3rd of July... | Calvin Hamilton to A.M. Apsted, 10 October, 1892.

%basis Eugene Arus Nash, A history of the Forty-fourth Regiment New York Volunteer Infantry in the Civil War (Chicago, 1911), p. 326 (note 32 of the report page 125)
125 | earthworks | Small breastwork thrown up by the 44th New York on the stony side hill | E-14 | 1863-07-02 (account published 1911) | stated | A letter of a Forty-fourth New York veteran, quoted by the report from the regimental history of 1911, says the brigade lay on a side hill covered with large stones and threw up a small breast-work for protection | =Our brigade lay on the side hill. It was covered with large stones. We threw up a small breast-work to protect us. | Eugene Arus Nash. >>> 1911. p. 326.

%basis Isaac O. Best, History of the 121st New York State Infantry (Chicago, 1921), p. 89 (note 33 of the report page 125)
125 | woodlots | Narrow strip of woods with scattered large rocks near Little Round Top | - | 1863-07-02 (account published 1921) | stated | The regimental history of the 121st New York (1921), quoted by the report, says the regiment occupied an advanced position under cover of a narrow strip of woods along which were scattered a number of large rocks | =[The regiment] occupied an advanced position under cover of a narrow strip of woods, along which were scattered a number of large rocks. | Isaac O. Best. >>> 1921. p.89.

%basis Ellis Spear, "Recollections", op. cit. (note 35 of the report page 125)
125 | woodlots | Bushes, chiefly small pines, among a scattered grove of oak trees on the Vincent Spur | - | 1863-07-02 (recollection written after the war) | stated | Major Ellis Spear of the 20th Maine, quoted by the report, says the 83rd Pennsylvania on the right of the 20th was not visible by reason of the bushes, chiefly small pines amongst the larger and more scattered grove of oak trees | =Apparently our line was not long enough to reach over and occupy Big Round Top so that our left was refused. The 83rd Penna. was on the right of the 20th but not visible by reason of the bushes, chiefly small pines amongst the larger and more scattered grove of oak trees. | =Major Ellis Spear, of the 20th Maine Infantry, described the thick vegetation that separated his regiment from those to his right.

%basis Theodore Gerrish, "Battle of Gettysburg", in Richard A. Sauers, ed., Fighting them Over (Baltimore, 1998) (note 36 of the report page 126)
126 | woodlots | Southern base of Little Round Top sparsely covered with oaks too small to give protection | - | 1863-07-02 (account of a 20th Maine veteran) | stated | A veteran of Company H, 20th Maine, quoted by the report, says the ground sloped to the front and left and was sparsely covered with a growth of oak trees too small to afford any protection | =The ground sloped to our front and left and was sparsely covered with a growth of oak trees, which were too small to afford us any protection. | =A veteran of Company H, 20th Maine Infantry described the condition of the landscape at the southern base of Little Round Top on the afternoon of July 2, 1863.

%basis Maine Gettysburg Commissioner's Executive Committee, Maine at Gettysburg (Portland, 1898), pp. 254, 257 (note 37 of the report page 126)
126 | woodlots | Smooth, thinly wooded valley between the Round Tops with shrubbery and rocks in the bottom | - | 1863-07-02 (account published 1898) | stated | Maine at Gettysburg (1898), quoted by the report, describes a smooth, thinly wooded valley between the two hills, the Alabama regiments forming their ranks in the shrubbery and rocks in the bottom of the valley | =a smooth, thinly wooded valley... [The 15th and 47th Alabama regiments formed their ranks] in the shrubbery and rocks in the bottom of the valley. | Maine Gettysburg Commissioner’s Executive Committee. >>> 1898. pp. 254, 257.

%basis Walter G. Morrill to Joshua Chamberlain, 8 July 1863, from the John G. Bachelder Papers, pp. 1029-30 (note 38 of the report page 126)
126 | fences | Stone wall east of Little Round Top used by Company B, 20th Maine, and U.S. Sharpshooters | P-14 | 1863-07-02 (letter of 8 July 1863) | stated | Capt. Walter G. Morrill, in a letter of 8 July 1863 to Chamberlain quoted by the report, says that after arriving at the left of the woods where he found twelve or fifteen U.S. Sharpshooters his command took position behind a stone wall there | =Having arrived on the field at the left of the woods, I found some twelve or fifteen U.S. Sharpshooters under the command of a non-commissioned officer, and he asked leave to remain under my command during the battle. We took position behind a stone wall there... | Walter G. Morrill to Joshua Chamberlain, 8 July, 1863.

%basis Report of Lieut. Col. A. Bryan, 5th Texas Infantry, Report No. 456, 8 July 1863 (note 39 of the report page 126)
126 | fences | Stone fence on the way up the hill taken by the 5th Texas | - | 1863-07-02 (report of 8 July 1863) | stated | Lieut. Col. Bryan's report of 8 July 1863 for the 5th Texas, quoted by the report, says the enemy retired to the top of the first height protected by a ledge of rocks, that a short halt was made at the stone fence, and that every man leaped the fence and advanced up the hillside | =At our approach, the enemy retired to the top of the first height, protected by a ledge of rocks. A short halt was made at the stone fence, to enable those who had fallen behind to regain their places. When the command ‘Forward’ again fell from the lips of our gallant colonel, every man leaped the fence, and advanced rapidly up the hillside. | Report of Lieut. Col. A Bryan, 5th Texas Infantry. >>> 8 July, 1863.

%basis -
128 | fences | Battle-era features of the Little Round Top project area extant in 2010-11 | - | ~2010-11 (integrity assessment of the report; locations only) | stated | The report's integrity assessment lists as battle-era extant features the Wheatfield Road alignment, fence line locations, prominent rock outcrops and the Munshower field; for the fences the report says elsewhere that most have been reconstructed, so only the locations are battle-era | ={{BaƩle|Battle}} Era Extant Features • Wheatfield Road (alignment) • Fencelines (location) • Prominent Rock Outcrops • Munshower Field
128 | roads | Wheatfield Road in place in 1863 and used as an avenue of approach | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report states that Wheatfield Road was in place during the battle and that several accounts exist of the roadway being used as an avenue of approach for troops during fighting | =Furthermore, Wheatfield Road was in place during the Battle of Gettysburg and several accounts exist of that roadway being used as an avenue of approach for troops during fighting.
